.Dd November 13, 2026
.Dt PR 1
.Os Substrate
.Sh NAME
.Nm pr
.Nd paginate or columnate files for printing
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Cm + Ns Ar page
.Op Fl Ar column
.Op Fl adFfmprt
.Op Fl e Ns Oo Ar char Oc Ns Op Ar gap
.Op Fl h Ar header
.Op Fl i Ns Oo Ar char Oc Ns Op Ar gap
.Op Fl l Ar lines
.Op Fl n Ns Oo Ar char Oc Ns Op Ar width
.Op Fl o Ar offset
.Op Fl s Ns Op Ar char
.Op Fl w Ar width
.Op Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility writes each
.Ar file
to standard output in pages.
With no
.Ar file
operands, or for an operand of
.Sq - ,
it reads standard input.
.Pp
A page is 66 lines: a header of 5 lines, the text, blank lines to fill
the page, and a trailer of 5 blank lines.
The header's third line is
.Pp
.Dl Ar date file Li Page Ar n
.Pp
where
.Ar date
is the file's modification time
(the current time for standard input and with
.Fl m ) ,
written as
.Ql %b %e %H:%M %Y
in
.Xr strftime 3 ,
and
.Ar file
is the operand, or empty for standard input and with
.Fl m .
The last page of a file is padded to the full length.
An empty file writes nothing.
.Pp
A form feed in the input ends the page; a newline right after it is part
of it.
With
.Fl t
the form feed is written instead.
.Pp
The input is written a page at a time as it is read; it is not held
whole.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm + Ns Ar page
Begin output at page
.Ar page
of each file.
.It Fl Ar column
Write
.Ar column
text columns, the input written down each in turn.
The columns are of equal width,
.Ar width
less the separators, divided by
.Ar column ;
each line is cut to its column's width.
A page's columns are balanced: as short as its lines allow, and never
longer than the page.
.Fl e
and
.Fl i
are assumed.
It cannot be used with
.Fl m .
.It Fl a
With
.Fl Ar column ,
fill the columns across the page: the first line heads the first
column, the second the second, and so on.
.It Fl d
Double-space the output: a blank line after each line.
A line and its blank line are written on one page.
.It Fl e Ns Oo Ar char Oc Ns Op Ar gap
Expand each input tab to the next column that is a multiple of
.Ar gap ,
8 if it is 0 or not given.
If
.Ar char ,
a non-digit, is given, it is the input tab.
The argument must be attached to the option.
.It Fl F
End each page with a form feed rather than the blank lines and the
trailer.
.It Fl f
As
.Fl F ,
and if standard output is a terminal, pause before the first page as
.Fl p
does.
.It Fl h Ar header
Write
.Ar header
in the page header in place of the file name.
.It Fl i Ns Oo Ar char Oc Ns Op Ar gap
In the output, replace spaces with tabs wherever one or more of them
reach a column that is a multiple of
.Ar gap ,
8 if it is 0 or not given.
If
.Ar char ,
a non-digit, is given, it is written in place of a tab.
The page header is left alone.
The argument must be attached to the option.
.It Fl l Ar lines
Pages of
.Ar lines
lines.
At 10 or fewer, there is no header or trailer, as with
.Fl t .
.It Fl m
Merge the files: one line of each, side by side, in text columns of
equal width, until every file has ended.
.Fl e
and
.Fl i
are assumed.
.It Fl n Ns Oo Ar char Oc Ns Op Ar width
Number the lines: a number of
.Ar width
digits (5 if not given; its last
.Ar width
digits if longer), then
.Ar char ,
a non-digit (a tab if not given).
In columns, each column's lines are numbered; with
.Fl m ,
each output line.
The argument must be attached to the option, the
.Ar char
before the
.Ar width :
.Fl n Ns Li 3
is a width of 3,
.Fl n Ns Li :3
a colon and a width of 3.
.It Fl o Ar offset
Write
.Ar offset
spaces before each line that is not empty, in addition to the width.
.It Fl p
If standard output is a terminal, before each page write an alert to
standard error and wait for a newline from
.Pa /dev/tty .
.It Fl r
Write no diagnostic for a file that cannot be opened.
The exit status still reports it.
.It Fl s Ns Op Ar char
Separate text columns with the one character
.Ar char
(a tab if not given) instead of padding them with spaces.
The default
.Ar width
becomes 512.
The character must be attached to the option.
.It Fl t
Write no header or trailer, and do not fill the last page.
.It Fl w Ar width
Lines of
.Ar width
columns for multi-column output, 72 by default.
Single-column lines are never cut.
.El
.Pp
Options may be grouped, as in
.Fl at ,
and an option-argument attached, as in
.Fl l Ns Li 50 .
The options come before the operands.
.Pp
Widths are display widths: a character is as wide as
.Xr wcwidth 3
says in the locale of
.Ev LC_CTYPE ,
a byte that is no character one column, a control character none.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width LC_CTYPE
.It Ev LC_CTYPE
The characters of the input, and their widths.
.It Ev LC_TIME
The date in the page header.
.It Ev TZ
The time zone of that date.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
A file that cannot be read is diagnosed, and the other files are still
written.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Pages of 50 lines headed
.Dq Report :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ pr -l 50 -h Report file1 file2
.Ed
.Pp
Three columns, numbered, with no headers:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ pr -t -3 -n file
.Ed
.Pp
Two files side by side, separated by a colon:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ pr -t -m -s: left right
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Each diagnostic begins
.Ql pr: .
While standard output is a terminal they are held until the end, so
that they do not land among the pages.
A failure to write standard output is diagnosed, and
.Nm
exits.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr paste 1 ,
.Xr wcwidth 3
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility conforms to IEEE Std 1003.1-2024
.Pq Dq POSIX.1-2024 .
